;. /authoring_wiki_flow
  ;@ /flow/definition authoring_wiki
  ;= Authoring in Shrine
    This source authors a wiki, not a mirror of repository READMEs. Publish
    authoring_wiki, bind its declared sources, and select the Nenex writer with
    a destination such as /0x11/app/nenex/w/authoring_wiki. No provider is needed.
    Each concept is its own document. Literal examples below remain inert.

    Attach maintenance to update the evidence index when sources change. The
    prose is deliberately authored here: a source change does not automatically
    establish that these instructions remain correct. Review the affected pages,
    edit this Semidoc, publish deliberately, then adopt the selected definition.
    Use the existing revision flow when a provider should propose prose changes.

    ;@ /flow/input sources
    ;= Captured implementation and authoring references

    ;@ /flow/step checked ;/0x11/gov/flows/operations/inventory
    ;@ /flow/bind sources sources
    ;= Require available identified evidence

    ;@ /flow/step pages ;/0x11/gov/flows/operations/evidence
    ;@ /flow/bind pages document
    ;@ /flow/bind sources checked
    ;= Project authored concepts and a compact evidence index

    ;@ /flow/result pages
    ;= Authoring wiki tree

    ;@ /flow/page /
    ;@ /flow/uses grove mounts authoring harness
    ;= Authoring in Shrine
      Build records that hold the domain, views that present those records,
      operations that admit changes, and flows that compose useful procedures.
      Nenex is the wiki in which people and agents can explain, inspect and
      invoke that work. It is not a second world beside the namespace.

      Read Start here for a short route through a working app. Grove covers
      records, contracts and interaction. Cells and overlays covers derived
      information. Hands covers reaction and shared work. Procedures covers
      Semidoc, composition and extraction. Mounting covers packaging and explicit
      publication. Verification covers what to check before claiming success.
      The reference children identify exact source captures without dumping the
      source text into the wiki. Inspect the work's evidence cells for the bytes.

    ;@ /flow/page start
    ;@ /flow/uses grove hello drafts authoring
    ;= Start here: a useful app without a second runtime
      Choose one concrete record, such as a field note with title and content.
      Give its cohesive values typed slots, define a matching role, and add a
      reading view and a native edit control. Seed two disposable examples.
      Compile a draft, inspect its actual preview and edit one sample through
      the declared action. Read the value back before claiming the app works.

      grove_source proposes source through an explicitly selected provider.
      grove_app_authoring composes that proposal with native draft creation and
      checks the exact compiled job. Exercise preview controls separately with
      invoke_and_verify; publish_grove_draft publishes a reviewed revision.
      Each stage returns native values. The source surface is at the current node's
      app/grove_authoring record; its new_draft and editor views are published
      under gov/grove_authoring. Create a named draft, inspect its job, and use
      its preview. Publish only the exact reviewed ready revision.
      ;/0x11/app/grove_authoring
      ;/0x11/gov/grove_authoring/new_draft
      ;/0x11/gov/grove_authoring/editor

      For a checkout-based app, hello-grove is a complete reference. Use a mount
      manifest to publish declarations, and instantiate sample data separately.
      Prefer a declarative Goo workspace when all the browser route needs is a
      path receiver and the shared shell. Do not start with custom JavaScript.

    ;@ /flow/page grove
    ;@ /flow/uses grove hello goo
    ;= Grove: declarations over native records
      A slot names a property. A role describes the required slots and codecs of
      a receiver. A view presents a matching receiver for a presentation slot.
      A pane composes interaction such as selection, followers and local ports.
      An action declares input and receiver contracts and computes or admits a
      change. An @tree declaration supplies installable sample records.

      Keep declaration roots and instance roots distinct. For hello-grove,
      gov/hello_grove contains published definitions, app/hello_grove contains
      installed notes, and io/fs/hello-grove is the mount source/control record.
      The same published role can describe many records. A presentation is a
      view of an existing subject; it does not make a parallel application model.

    ;@ /flow/page grove/records
    ;@ /flow/uses grove hello
    ;= Records, slots and children
      Put properties of one entity in its record. A postal address usually
      belongs in a cohesive typed Foil value or an explicit domain role. Turning
      every scalar into a child document loses that cohesion and creates work
      for identity, ownership and rendering. Use child records when a value
      needs its own identity, independent edits, links or work lifecycle.

      Source format is not domain design. Decode a CSV row, JSON object or OSM
      feature into an appropriate native value, retain the original capture,
      then apply an explicit domain mapping. An OSM identifier, geometry,
      address tags and normalization evidence should not be conflated. Preserve
      source identifiers, missing values and provenance. Current bundled format
      decoding supports JSON, Markdown and text; it is not an OSM or CSV decoder.

      This minimal Grove example uses two existing system slots. The sample
      path is relative to the installed instance; %/note is relative to the
      published declaration root.
      ;$ grove
        #import text=lib/grove/types/text
        #import lede=sys/lede
        #import content=sys/content

        note =
          @role
          lede: text
          content: text

        "/notes/first" =
          @tree
          lede: "First note"
          content: "An ordinary native record."

    ;@ /flow/page grove/codecs
    ;@ /flow/uses grove hello mounts
    ;= Codecs and stable contracts
      Import the installed text, path and other appropriate codecs. A custom
      Grove @type wraps a native type with read, write and contract definitions.
      Its decoder must reject incompatible pails; its encoder must preserve
      the intended value. Declare a domain struct when several fields form one
      value. Do not make strings impersonate typed references or native work.

      Persistent records retain the types with which they were created. When
      separating a reusable contract from executable helpers, pin the exact
      contract module artifact and publish implementation modules separately.
      Similar source text is not evidence of identical type identity. Retained
      imports and explicit publication predecessors decide which artifacts are
      available; publication does not discover replacements by matching names.

    ;@ /flow/page grove/views
    ;@ /flow/uses grove hello goo
    ;= Reading views and native editing
      Bind a view's receiver with #on and its purpose with #for. show renders
      a typed value through Goo. group carries an action binding; .input values
      are buffered form inputs, and action submits the bound native operation.
      Browser layout and paint may use the shared renderer, but the receiver,
      argument schema, admission and resulting records remain native.

      A simple record editor can use .poke. Use a domain action when an invariant
      needs validation or a change involves more than straightforward fields.
      Never implement the authoritative domain validation only in browser code.
      ;$ grove
        edit =
          @view
          #on ; %/note
          #for ; /sys/slot/note_edit
          note => group(on = note, action = .poke, myth = {
            '/sys/lede = .input(note.lede)
            '/sys/content = .input(note.content)
          }) {
            input('/sys/lede)
            input('/sys/content)
            action("Save")
          }

    ;@ /flow/page grove/actions
    ;@ /flow/uses grove authoring drafts
    ;= Actions, admission and receipts
      Declare the inputs and receiver role of an @action. A normal typed action
      can return its next receiver. For a namespace operation, the action's
      /sys/op metadata points to the existing namespace_op planner. That planner
      validates against the supplied state and returns native writes; shared
      admission settles their reactions or rejects the candidate transaction.
      The draft source controls are a small real example of this pattern.

      Discover actual controls with shrine_actions or the corresponding Goo
      inspector. Keep the selected receiver, view, control, argument contract
      and version guards together. Submit with shrine_act or the same rendered
      Goo control. Rediscover after changing the receiver or relevant evidence.
      Read the receipt and resulting record. An HTTP success or a model report
      is not proof of a completed domain change.

      Action scope and provider authority are different. A task brief instructs
      the agent; it does not sandbox a provider with namespace authority. Choose
      explicit providers, validate operations, and make irreversible or external
      effects separate deliberate steps. Retries must inspect earlier receipts.

    ;@ /flow/page grove/panes
    ;@ /flow/uses grove hello goo
    ;= Lists, selection and composed panes
      A selection owner connects a source to named local selection state. A
      list presents the source's members; a follower presents the selected
      subject. Child panes receive selection ports explicitly. This allows a
      notes list, reader and editor to cooperate without copying records into
      frontend stores. The hello-grove example contains the complete pattern.

      Choose presentation slots deliberately: summary, detail and edit are
      different purposes, not different identities of the note. A view can
      become another presentation after an action while keeping the subject.
      Inspect the real recipe and receiver when selection is unavailable;
      do not manufacture a selection from a DOM label or guessed namespace path.

    ;@ /flow/page computed_values
    ;@ /flow/uses overlays authoring harness
    ;= Cells: values with explicit dependencies
      A cell is a native record holding a constructor and inputs. Its ordinary
      path exposes that definition. The /x projection exposes the computed tree;
      /c/x exposes the computed context. Those computed views are not separately
      retained records. A work capture can freeze the value deliberately in an
      ordinary cell using a pinned tree, so an attempt keeps its original inputs.

      The cell constructor receives its own slots and hydrated secondary input
      views, plus the tack input tree, and returns context and data. Bind a
      downstream operation to the value it needs. Binding a raw capture cell is
      different from binding its projection: extraction and handoff require the
      raw native_work/captured record, including its historical reference.

      Prefer existing constructors and published pure operations. A cell is not
      an I/O loop. A source foot performs external I/O, a source hand admits the
      current evidence, and cells interpret it. A hand is used when a reaction
      must persist a change or emit work. Keep that boundary visible.

    ;@ /flow/page overlays
    ;@ /flow/uses overlays authoring
    ;= Overlay constructors and working interpretations
      The native vocabulary distinguishes knit, felt, dyed, sewn, spun and cell.
      Knit derives a tree from a record; felt reduces a tree to a record; dyed
      transforms record content; sewn transforms tree structure. These four
      include optional patch laws. Spun derives structure from its own argument
      record and tack tree, with an optional patch law. Cell also returns derived
      context. These are actual constructor contracts, not names for six stores.

      /sys/weft selects the constructor inline or by its supported reference;
      /sys/tack selects the main input. The native view evaluator owns reference
      resolution and dependency/cycle behavior. Compose published constructors
      instead of adding an application-specific resolver or a new namespace axis.

      A work overlay can present a tentative document, evidence, step results
      and unresolved inputs together. Reading that overlay does not publish the
      tentative result. The native writer and its guards decide whether it can
      become current. Retain the underlying captures and attempts for inspection.

    ;@ /flow/page hands
    ;@ /flow/uses hands cooperate harness
    ;= Hands: reactions over the same namespace
      A native form has arms; a record's behavior slot holds a kook naming
      published form records. Its crew maps local dependency names to care/path
      pairs. The supplied bowl carries the receiver path, activation context,
      in-flight children and hydrated committed dependency views. An arm returns
      a yell containing its next state and emitted native writes.

      Maintenance, answering, context-lens, source-feed and provider hands are
      application roles built with this vocabulary. They are not five separate
      schedulers or five incompatible record models. First look for an existing
      shared hand that performs the transition you need. A new pure conversion
      usually belongs in a flow operation or cell constructor, not a new hand.

    ;@ /flow/page hands/arms
    ;@ /flow/uses hands
    ;= The native arms and their inputs
      init receives bowl and state. talk additionally receives an input record.
      take receives a tree of add/dif/del changes. hear receives changed cared
      paths from declared dependencies. Each returns a yell of cards and state.
      dead and goof exist in the form type but are vocabulary only in this port;
      they are not invoked. Do not depend on them for cleanup or recovery.

      The bowl's now is a logical activation clock, not wall time. Its kids view
      is the node's in-flight care-y tree. Its crew contains committed dependency
      views under local names, allowing the watched paths to change independently
      of the law's local field names. Declare the care and path you actually need.

      make replaces a record, poke merges fields, cull deletes the current node,
      and warp applies a declared operation using transient input. Historical
      versions are not an excuse to overwrite unrelated current work. Allocate
      new attempts only through the existing guards and retain prior attempts.

    ;@ /flow/page hands/dependencies
    ;@ /flow/uses hands cooperate harness mounts
    ;= Dependencies, source ownership and cleanup
      crew is the native subscription map. A hand should return the current
      dependencies, dropping obsolete live subscriptions while keeping retained
      captures, attempts and provenance. Do not add a parallel list of watches
      or poll from the flow. Source adapters and the existing filesystem foot
      already reconcile ownership and invalidate obsolete polling workers.

      Two flows can share the same captured filesystem source. Detaching one
      must not stop the other's watch; detaching the last must release it and
      prevent late watcher results from publishing. A missing file is unavailable
      current evidence, even when its former bytes remain in retained history.
      Restoration can wake the same dependencies and continue unresolved work.

      Keep exact work/head and generation guards when answering a hole. A stale
      answer must not complete a newer request. Cleanup affects live registrations
      and current authority, not the historical evidence needed to understand
      what happened. Do not rely on dropping an actor handle as proof it stopped.

    ;@ /flow/page procedures
    ;@ /flow/uses harness authoring semidoc
    ;= Flows are authored procedures
      A flow makes inputs, steps, dependencies, results and effects explicit.
      It can be authored directly, composed from existing operations or flows,
      or extracted from retained work. Semidoc carries its explanation alongside
      marked executable regions. Ordinary prose and literal code remain inert.
      Nenex projects the same authored records as wiki documents and controls.

      A published definition captures selected operation laws and origins. An
      occurrence supplies bindings and a destination. An attempt retains that
      definition, its actual inputs, intermediate results and outcome. A provider
      may perform judgment inside a request boundary; that does not make the
      provider's decision deterministic. The surrounding dependency evaluation
      and native guards still have explicit behavior.

    ;@ /flow/page procedures/authoring
    ;@ /flow/uses harness authoring semidoc
    ;= Author and publish a small Semidoc procedure
      Mark the definition, inputs, steps, each binding and the result explicitly.
      Repeated /flow/bind attributes are distinct relationships and must survive
      both source and Goo editing. Select operations by published namespace
      address. The literal below is an example, not another flow in this guide.
      ;$ semidoc
        ;. /check_sources_flow
          ;@ /flow/definition check_sources
          ;= Check identified sources
            ;@ /flow/input sources
            ;= Explicit evidence
            ;@ /flow/step checked ;/0x11/gov/flows/operations/inventory
            ;@ /flow/bind sources sources
            ;= Validate source envelopes
            ;@ /flow/result checked
            ;= Validated sources

      Author the page in Nenex, then publish the selected definition by name.
      This example returns source data, so invoke it with a data-retaining writer
      or compose it into another procedure; the Nenex writer expects page values.
      Creation, publication, binding and attachment are separate decisions.
      The catalogue shortcut performs the selected ordinary operations together,
      but does not change their semantics or create a new execution environment.

    ;@ /flow/page procedures/composition
    ;@ /flow/uses harness authoring
    ;= Compose operations, children and occurrences
      /flow/step selects a published operation. /flow/invoke selects a published
      child definition. /flow/bind connects an input name to an earlier result,
      an explicit input or the authored document templates. Publish children
      before parents; the parent captures exact child definitions. Editing a
      child's source does not silently retarget an existing parent or attempt.

      Embed a published occurrence in another Nenex page with its own bindings.
      The page can expose its cells, work and holes without a parallel iframe
      app or duplicated lifecycle. Choose result shapes deliberately: source
      envelopes, cohesive typed data, document pages and interactive requests
      are different values. Connect compatible operations, not merely similar
      step labels. Preserve source identities through every transformation.

      A useful data chain selects configured queries, captures sources, decodes
      their declared formats, performs domain checks, then projects selected
      values into documents. The mapping and checks are normal published code
      or flows. A language model can author that procedure, but tests and explicit
      publication determine whether its proposed behavior should be used.

    ;@ /flow/page procedures/extraction
    ;@ /flow/uses authoring harness
    ;= Extract work without inventing execution
      A retained flow attempt already has executable steps, captured laws and
      their historical origins. Native extraction selects executed steps,
      preserves their dependency order and exposes omitted dependencies as
      explicit inputs with frozen examples. It does not re-resolve today's law
      merely because the namespace address has the same name.

      Investigation/model traces have observations and tool calls, not necessarily
      reusable executable operations. Native trace extraction retains selected
      events and evidence, authors an editable Semidoc draft, and keeps an
      authoring hole. Choose operations and dependencies, check the draft, then
      deliberately review and publish. The procedure_extraction recipe uses
      these exact controls; it does not turn arbitrary tool text into a program.

    ;@ /flow/page collaboration
    ;@ /flow/uses cooperate authoring harness
    ;= Shared context, holes and handoff
      A context lens binds the subject and selected evidence into a useful
      working view. Typed relationships can make relevant guidance discoverable
      through the existing scoped /r query. Ordinary Semidoc links do not by
      themselves create those typed relationships. Keep the scope explicit so
      unrelated notes do not feed back into the answer's own context.

      Persistent holes describe unresolved work attached to the subject. They
      can request evidence, a decision or a clarification. Answer through the
      declared control on that same work; its hand rechecks current evidence
      and guards. The UI derives active, waiting and blocked status from native
      work. It must not maintain an independent lifecycle in JavaScript.

      assess_work returns the original typed work, owner, as-of status and capture.
      investigation_handoff composes it with plan_continuation; project_result
      can present the proposal as a wiki. resume_work uses an explicitly selected
      action on the original work and verifies separately bound observations.
      The next participant rereads current state and continues that same work.
      A handoff report is neither new authority nor a substitute for the current
      hole generation. Earlier failed attempts remain inspectable.

    ;@ /flow/page mounting
    ;@ /flow/uses mounts grove
    ;= Mounts package declarations and optional transport
      A mount.json names dependencies, module files, Grove roots, source files,
      optional assets, tests and activation/HTTP hooks. A logical module can
      contain slashes, but mapped source filenames are flat within the mount's
      source bag. Declare every publication root the mount owns. Keep domain
      policy in the extra and reusable contracts in the existing native layers.

      The launcher stages bytes and starts the native runtime. It does not
      implement a flow scheduler. Prefer the declarative workspaces field for
      a path-receiver page. Use a host foot only for external I/O or process
      resources; admitted records, work state and reports belong in the namespace.
      Select mounts explicitly. The app authoring flow does not mutate the
      checkout manifest or install host dependencies on an agent's behalf.

      From the checkout root, these launch separate disposable namespaces.
      The first opens the existing Grove example; the second includes the
      authored library and its native controls. Add --mount codex to the second
      command when an interactive provider is wanted and configured.
      ;$ sh
        x/eden --mount hello-grove --ephemeral --port 8350
        x/eden --mount flow-examples --ephemeral --port 8351

      To package your own app, start with an extra's mount.json and Grove source,
      declare its dependencies and owned roots, and select its name with --mount.
      Use hello-grove/mount.json for installation plus a route, or the declarative
      workspace fragment below for a pane. A catalogue flow, a mounted source
      module and an installed app instance have different identities; name and
      inspect the one you are changing.

    ;@ /flow/page mounting/workspaces
    ;@ /flow/uses mounts goo
    ;= A declarative Goo workspace
      Declare explorer as a dependency. A workspaces entry selects a published
      pane, one path-valued receiver slot, an initial subject and a route. The
      entry and slot must be inside the mount's declared Grove roots. This is
      enough for ordinary native list/selection/follow interactions without an
      application-specific browser bundle or custom HTTP handler.
      ;$ json
        {"workspaces":[{"name":"main","route":"/notes",
          "entry":["gov","notes","workspace"],
          "receiver":{"slot":["gov","notes","source_path"],
            "initial":["app","notes"],"admission":"namespace"},
          "shell":{"title":"Notes","breadcrumbs":true}}]}

      This is a manifest fragment; supply the modules, Grove roots and required
      dependencies in the enclosing mount. Paths are symbolic arrays prefixed
      with the active node except sys paths. Workspace routes and assets are
      implicitly claimed; do not repeat them in routes. Receiver admission
      validates the subject's shape, not user authentication. A route request
      can select a canonical subject path, not arbitrary panes or receiver slots.

    ;@ /flow/page publication
    ;@ /flow/uses mounts drafts authoring
    ;= Publication, installation and deliberate adoption
      Publication makes executable declarations available. Installation applies
      a selected template to an instance. Capturing a definition for work keeps
      its exact implementation; it is not a standing request to follow every
      future publication. Updating a mounted behavior and replacing a captured
      service/compiler closure are different operations. Restart does not imply
      automatic upgrading of all retained definitions or runtime handlers.

      The Grove source draft host registers fixed preview and published roots
      using the existing grove_draft registration. Each source revision compiles
      into a candidate namespace. Failed compile/install leaves the last successful
      preview and records diagnostics. A ready revision is not automatically
      published. Explicit publish compiles and installs that exact revision into
      its separate published sample instance. Inspect its status and records.

      That sample replacement is not a data migration strategy. Do not publish
      new templates over an existing business instance and assume its edits will
      survive. Select and review the appropriate native installation/adoption
      operation, with destination guards. Keep contracts pinned and behavior
      updates explicit; do not patch a saved bootstrap to disguise an upgrade.

    ;@ /flow/page mounting/snapshots
    ;@ /flow/uses mounts
    ;= Native snapshots and external reconnection
      Eden is disposable by default. Select --snapshot PATH explicitly for a
      retained native arena and repeat the mount selection on restart. Native
      Save retains reachable namespace state and history, including captured
      definitions. The commit boundary saves accepted changes before acknowledging
      them. External process handles, sockets and feet need reconnection; that
      does not justify another application save/export format.

      Do not promise automatic storage reclamation. Native arena compaction is
      currently an explicit offline prototype maintenance strategy. It preserves
      the reachable world and retained history but requires downtime and temporary
      disk space. Automatic reclamation and its performance remain unresolved.
      Never compact or replace a live arena as part of a flow example.

    ;@ /flow/page verification
    ;@ /flow/uses mounts authoring drafts cooperate
    ;= Verify the native behavior you claim
      Start in a disposable namespace. Compile real authored source, inspect
      published roles and recipes, then invoke a declared action and read back
      the resulting record. Exercise invalid input and a stale revision as well
      as the successful case. For drafts, verify that failed compilation keeps
      the old preview and cannot publish the failed revision.

      For flows, inspect captured definitions and inputs, step cells, suspension,
      holes, resumption and writer guards. For extraction, compare original and
      extracted law origins and bindings. For handoff, check that the original
      work is unchanged. For source watches, cover shared ownership, rebind,
      last-consumer detach, deletion, restoration and rejected late results.

      Register behavioral checks in the existing native test harness. Reports
      are namespace records. A controlled provider establishes orchestration
      behavior, not a fresh end-to-end model run. Compilation proves types and
      lowering, not business correctness. Browser reload proves neither server
      restart nor retained-arena recovery. State exactly which boundary passed.

    ;@ /flow/page troubleshooting
    ;@ /flow/uses mounts authoring harness drafts
    ;= Diagnose a stalled or refused operation
      Inspect current native work and its hole before changing timeouts. Waiting
      for input means a question or evidence is unresolved; blocked means the
      work reports a refusal or inability to continue; actively working means
      an admitted request is progressing. Follow the attempt and receipt rather
      than guessing from a spinner. Cold compilation can be expensive and is
      separate from warm action performance.

      If a draft has attempted equal to revision but ready is older, read the
      compile diagnostic. If a source feed is unavailable, inspect its current
      capture and ownership. If a guarded write refuses, reread the destination,
      current evidence and generation; do not bypass the guard or delete history.
      If a new publication does not change existing work, inspect what was
      captured and deliberately adopt the intended definition. Use existing
      cells, hands and overlays to expose the cause before adding infrastructure.
